AUX-CONTEXT|<work>/refute-out/run/aux_src_withdraws_AbstractWithdrawRequestManager_sol_getWithdrawRequestValue_322.txt
Tracing the actual resolved code: getWithdrawRequestValue reads s_accountWithdrawRequest[vault][account], and if not
  finalized prices w.yieldTokenAmount of YIELD_TOKEN via TRADING_MODULE.getOraclePrice(YIELD_TOKEN, asset). The claim's
  entire premise — that EthenaCooldownHolder._startCooldown burns sUSDe into a fixed USDe silo claim, so the yield-token
  rate diverges from the frozen entitlement — depends on Ethena-specific code that appears in NEITHER the abstract
  contract under review NOR the derived implementation supplied in the appendix. The only concrete implementation given
  is GenericERC20WithdrawRequestManager, constructed as AbstractWithdrawRequestManager(_erc20, _erc20, _erc20), i.e.
  WITHDRAW_TOKEN == YIELD_TOKEN == STAKING_TOKEN; there both branches of the valuation use the same token, the same
  oracle pair, and _initiateWithdrawImpl merely records s_withdrawRequestTokens[requestId] = tokensToWithdraw with
  _finalizeWithdrawImpl returning that exact amount and finalized = true. No freezing of an entitlement, no rate
  divergence between branches, and no accrual to over-borrow against exists in the resolved behaviour actually shown.

  Even granting the unshown Ethena integration arguendo, the claimed divergence is the yield accrual of the staking
  token across a bounded, protocol-fixed cooldown window — a sub-percent, monotonically-capped drift in reported
  collateral value, not an unbounded manipulation lever an unprivileged actor controls. The attacker cannot lengthen the
  cooldown, cannot amplify the rate, and cannot choose when the divergence starts beyond calling initiateWithdraw
  (itself gated by onlyApprovedVault — the vault, not the holder, is msg.sender, and the request is one-per-account via
  if (accountWithdraw.requestId != 0) revert ExistingWithdrawRequest(...)). Selecting between the finalized and
  not-finalized branch is also not attacker-controlled in a profitable direction: anyone may call finalizeRequestManual,
  which flips s.finalized = true and locks the valuation to totalWithdraw, so the "stale high" branch is
  permissionlessly collapsible by the very liquidators the claim says are blocked.

  The step that would have to break an invariant — a freeze of the underlying claim while the oracle used keeps moving —
  is simply not present in either file provided, and the one implementation provided makes the two branches numerically
  equivalent. Under the tie-break rule this cannot survive.

  CONSTRAINT|class=C15|A claim that a not-yet-settled position is mispriced by a live rate must point to code within the
  reviewed scope that actually freezes the underlying entitlement at request time, and must show a divergence the
  unprivileged actor can control or amplify rather than a bounded accrual over a protocol-fixed window that any party
  can permissionlessly collapse.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/withdraws/AbstractWithdrawRequestManager.sol:getWithdrawRequestValue:322|class=C15|The freezing
  behaviour the claim rests on exists in neither the abstract contract nor the supplied derived implementation, whose
  constructor sets WITHDRAW_TOKEN == YIELD_TOKEN so both valuation branches price the same token at the same oracle, and
  any party may call the permissionless finalizeRequestManual to set s.finalized = true and lock the value to
  totalWithdraw.
